{"id": "feaeba01-df56-42d3-8ce2-a255ece4efbe", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm seeing several failures all tracing back to the same root cause—placeholder instance IDs like i-1234567890abcdef0_1 being used instead of real ones, affecting both standalone runs and their child processes.

The parents (5dce3b7f and ee671768) failed as a consequence of their children failing rather than their own direct error, since there's no FailureMessage on them. With MaxErrors=1 on 5dce3b7f, both child failures pushed it over the limit, while ee671768's MaxErrors=0 meant it stopped at the first failure. These IDs look like documentation placeholder values rather than real instances, which is likely why EC2 is rejecting them as malformed.

"}]}
